dge/offset decoder, clipping and zero-query prior; Schur-complement rationale is an algebraic derivation, not an empirical component claim.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L6, source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L31, source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L75\n33: Source counterpart lineage-disjoint refitting/tuning, two folds and 5/10/25 prefixes; optional outer nested decoder tuning.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L111, source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L142\n34: Source counterpart optional log-odds shift, prefix-matched residual ridge and linear fallback; per-case activation and isolated benefit unverified.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L18, source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L65\n35: Settings cap 4 and fitting allowance 200; listed capped hyperparameters, 64 shift iterations, bounded shifts and clipping constants describe supplied counterpart, not selected final branch diagnostics.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L18, source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L129, source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L111, source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L930\n36: Local baselines: content-group-mass weighted clipped uniform estimator, constant prior, normalized response embeddings/farthest-point anchors, logistic IRT with 200 source and 40 target updates and clipped initialization. Not exact published systems.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L580, source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L598\n37: Lineage-weighted MAE and RMSE, lineage-mean rank correlations, average of cell RMSE rather than pooled RMSE.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L556, source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L1014, source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L1040\n38: Known-lineage and common-stem safeguards are metadata-limited and do not prove undocumented ancestry or pretraining independence; retained-column scores differ from unreconciled originals.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/manifests/bbh_manifest.json#/limitations, source_evidence/manifests/bbh_manifest.json#/normalization\n39: Readable selection counterpart is supplied, but byte-for-byte source-tree identity is not independently certified. Digests are provenance, not substantive implementation support.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=source_evidence/inventory.json, paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/program_tree_sha256\n40: No controlled component ablations, per-case branch activation evidence, or new-benchmark interval; conclusions concern recorded packages, not isolated effects or guaranteed transfer.\n  sources=final.evidence.episodes\n  refs=paper_data.json#/ablation_studies, paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust\n41: Literature/software context, limited to the consulted source scope: Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples\n  sources=ref.arxiv.2309.08638\n  refs=literature_evidence/Vivek2024.html\n42: Literature/software context, limited to the consulted source scope: tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\n  sources=ref.arxiv.2402.14992\n  refs=literature_evidence/Polo2024.html\n43: Literature/software context, limited to the consulted source scope: metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models\n  sources=ref.arxiv.2407.12844\n  refs=literature_evidence/Kipnis2025.html\n44: Literature/software context, limited to the consulted source scope: 100 instances is all you need: predicting the success of a new LLM on unseen data by testing on a few instances\n  sources=ref.arxiv.2409.03563\n  refs=literature_evidence/Pacchiardi2024.html\n45: Literatur